# Refactor One Path

Use this rule when the user supplies one concrete file or directory path for refactoring. Preserve observable behavior while making feature and role boundaries explicit.

## Required workflow

1. Resolve the supplied path, read every applicable `AGENTS.md`, inspect Git status, and preserve pre-existing edits.
2. Inspect the complete target boundary: source roots, build manifests, generated-source boundaries, callers, callees, registrations, documentation, and focused tests.
3. Run the smallest useful baseline check before editing and record failures that already exist.
4. Refactor directly without pausing for ordinary design confirmation. Update all in-repository callers and configuration references in the same change.
5. Delete only code proven unused by references, build analysis, or tests. Do not add deprecated wrappers, forwarding shells, aliases, or compatibility protocols.
6. Keep a user-visible feature cohesive across transport, service, model, adapter, UI, tests, and documentation. Keep genuinely reusable infrastructure in its own boundary and document the relationship.

## Role boundaries

Use feature-first directories with only roles that contain real code:

- `controller`: transport orchestration only; parse input, call a service, and prepare output.
- `model`: DTOs, requests, responses, value objects, and domain data; no I/O or orchestration.
- `service`: boundary interfaces consumed by another boundary or host.
- `service/impl`: service implementations; keep implementation visibility narrow.
- `assist`: independently meaningful stateless parsing, mapping, rendering, normalization, or algorithm collaborators.
- `constants`: actively referenced constants only.
- `enums`: enum declarations.
- `adapter`: database, network, framework, filesystem, and external-system integration.

Keep models, service interfaces, and implementations in separate files. Keep private one-use helpers with their owner. Controllers must not own persistence, conversion, or domain decisions. Do not add generic `api`, `common`, or `utils` buckets.

## Dependency and extension rules

Prefer `Controller -> Service/Model` and `ServiceImpl -> Assist/Adapter/SPI`. Extract an SPI only when implementations cross a module or discovery boundary. Name SPI contracts with an `Spi` suffix, concrete implementations with an `Impl` suffix, and place them in `spi-<feature>` and `impl-<feature>-<technology>` modules. Keep SPI modules framework-free and free of implementation dependencies.

Respect generated-source boundaries: change generators, metadata, registrations, or compiler sources instead of repeatable output. Reduce visibility after moving code. Preserve the repository's explicit architecture and persistence contracts.

## Verification

Search for stale paths and names, unresolved callers, duplicate contracts, dependency cycles, missing feature documentation, mixed model/logic files, and unused declarations. Run focused tests, affected builds, `cargo fmt --all --check` where applicable, and `git diff --check`. Report exact blocked commands, pre-existing failures, and functional defects intentionally left unchanged.
